{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>The ruins of Abarim.</b> I do not know why their name is called עִיִּים. And עִי is a term for a heap of ruins — a thing swept away with a broom. The עַיִ״ן in it is yod alone, and it is from the root of ״יָעִים״ (Exodus 27), ״וְיָעָה בָרָד״ (Isaiah 28)." "<b>Of Abarim.</b> The route of those who cross there past Mount Nebo into the land of Canaan — for it separates between the land of Moab and the land of the Amorite." "<b>On the face of Moab, toward the east of the sun.</b> To the east of the land of Moab."], :book :numbers, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "Rashi cites his uncertainty about the toponym's etymology; the root discussion concerns whether עִי here derives from עַיִ״ן-yod or relates to forms spelled with yod. ׳דָּבָר הַטָּאוּט בְּמַטְאֲטֵא׳ — literally ׳a thing swept with a broom׳ — illustrates ruin/desolation."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:11:56.709946942Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}